  "notes": "Topline writer and session vocalist central to the EDM-pop-into-pop crossover. She is not a programmer either, but the entry gives three concrete drum facts about the records she writes for: \"a sparse kick-and-clap with the low end used sparingly and a fill after every fourth bar — her toplines are written to leave that fill audible\", over \"a four-on-the-floor or trop-house bed\". So `fills.smallEveryBars` is 4 with `fillBeforeSection` true, the kick is a four-square at a low density with the perc bed carrying the trop-house offbeats, and the snare is a plain clap stack at a tight offset. Leaving the fill audible is why the melody carries a real `emptyBarBias` — the topline gets out of its way. The bass claim is the second usable one: \"her toplines characteristically sit above a moving funk bass line rather than a pad\", which is `independent_riff` at 0.3 with a full passing-tone set and walking cells; no `bass808` block is authored because none is described. Harmony is documented from the specific record she co-wrote: \"minor-mode loops with 7ths; the disco-facing work she co-wrote is B minor in 4/4, and her habit is a chorus that stays inside the verse's harmonic frame rather than lifting to a new one\" — so B minor is in `keys`, `seventh` is high, and the family set is deliberately short because verse and chorus share it. The melody block follows her own account: \"she sings lead and background on the records she writes, so the melodies are pitched for a real performance rather than an ideal one — mid-register, narrow-span, with melisma at phrase ends and no modulation\", which is a mid register of barely a tenth, `endVariation` for the melisma and no modulation device anywhere in the model. \"A call-and-response ad-lib layer over the final chorus\" is the `answer_lick` counter. ⚠ `confidence` is medium against a medium-high rating; every drum probability, the humanize pair, `bpm.mode` 114 (the midpoint of the stated 100—128 band) and `session.keys` beyond its first value are INFERRED — the entry names only \"the disco-facing work she co-wrote is B minor\", so A minor, F♯ minor and E minor are inferred neighbouring minor tonics and appear nowhere in the research.",
